}

' Dependencies the automated pass cannot infer: it reads the members but not what
' they are used for. workflow_registry and workflow_definition are deliberately not
' drawn here -- they belong to ores.workflow.api, whose own diagram carries them,
' and a cross-module edge would draw them again as empty boxes.

"ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine" --> "ores::workflow::service::fsm_state_map" : resolves state names
"ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_instance_repository"
"ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_step_repository"
"ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_query_handler" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_instance_repository"
"ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_query_handler" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_step_repository"
"ores::workflow::repository::workflow_instance_repository" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_instance_mapper"
"ores::workflow::repository::workflow_step_repository" --> "ores::workflow::repository::workflow_step_mapper"

' The composition point. register_handlers is the only place a workflow handler is
' subscribed, and it folds all four generated families.

"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::register_workflow_instance_handlers" : folds
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::register_workflow_step_handlers" : folds
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::register_workflow_instance_history_provider" : folds
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::register_workflow_step_history_provider" : folds
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine" : builds, starts recovery
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_query_handler" : subscribes four queries
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_handler" : subscribes the provision request
"ores::workflow::messaging::registrar" ..> "ores::workflow::service::load_fsm_states" : loads once at startup

note bottom of "ores::workflow::messaging::registrar"
Two families, two shapes. A message registrar subscribes
its own per-entity subjects and returns its own
subscriptions. A history provider registers on one shared
table instead, because a single generic subject serves
both entities, so the table has to outlive the one
subscription -- which is why it is a function-local
static rather than a local.
end note

note right of "ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine"
Every state change is persisted before the next command is
published, so a restart resumes in-flight instances from
recover_in_progress() rather than losing them. A step is
addressed by its step_id, which doubles as the idempotency
key that lets a domain service recognise a re-dispatched
command and answer with the result it already produced.

The engine resolves a workflow's type through the
workflow_registry it is handed. That type and its
workflow_definition belong to ores.workflow.api, so they are
drawn on that module's diagram rather than repeated here.
end note

note bottom of "ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_query_handler"
get_steps returns the step-log projection the shell prints.
A step can report success and still have skipped work, so
the log travels beside the status rather than inside the
error, and a reader that prints only the status cannot tell
a clean run from a lossy one.
end note

